Decode-shaped get_rows and concat run on ONE thread (27B, 2026-09-21).

An instrumented ggml-cpu built from the engine's own commit, profiling every
node of a shielded 27B decode, found 11.3 ms of an 87 ms token inside
GET_ROWS: 197 calls, mean 57 us, the worst gathering [786432,1,1,1] -- 3 MB
in ONE row. ggml partitions get_rows over the rows it gathers, so a gather of
a single row runs on thread zero while the rest wait at the barrier. A hybrid
model's recurrent state is exactly that shape at decode and is gathered
several times per delta-net layer.

CONCAT has the same shape of problem: it distributes over ne2, which is 1 for
a decode ubatch, and copies element by element through a four-way branch --
273 ms over the same run, worst instance conv_input [20, 10240].

get_rows_f32: when there are fewer rows than threads and the rows are long,
split the ROW across threads instead. The slices are disjoint, so it is the
same copy; the per-row path is untouched, which keeps prefill (many rows)
exactly as it was.

concat_f32: distribute over every row of (ne1, ne2, ne3) rather than ne2
alone. Each thread walks a contiguous range of rows carrying its own
(i1, i2, i3), so the index arithmetic costs three divisions per THREAD -- a
first version that recomputed them per row with a modulo was 3% SLOWER than
the stock kernel, which is what a per-row integer division buys you at 49
concats a token. Rows of 256 bytes or more are copied whole; shorter rows and
non-contiguous layouts keep the element loop and its source selection.

Measured on the 27B (two V100s, host loopback, ring transport): GET_ROWS
765 -> 310 ms over the run, the CPU-side graph 2184 -> 1516 ms, and decode
11.3 -> 12.5 tok/s plain, 12.5 -> 14.2 with one-token drafting.

